// ═══ yield() — what it does (or doesn't do) ═══
Thread.yield();
// "Dear scheduler, I'm done with my timeslice. Please consider running
//  other threads of equal priority."
// Scheduler response: "Sure, I'll check... nobody else ready. You keep going."
// OR: "OK, Thread-2 gets a turn."
// NO GUARANTEE EITHER WAY!

// ═══ WHY IT'S UNRELIABLE ═══
// 1. Scheduler may ignore: on modern OSes with preemptive scheduling,
//    the scheduler decides when to switch — yield is just noise.
// 2. Platform-dependent: Windows may honor it, Linux may not (or vice versa).
// 3. No timing guarantee: even if it yields, the yielding thread may be
//    rescheduled immediately (nanoseconds later).
// 4. Priority-dependent: only yields to threads of EQUAL or HIGHER priority.
//    If no such thread exists → no yield happens.

// ═══ WHEN yield() IS used (rare) ═══
// 1. Spin-wait loops (give CPU a break):
while (!condition.get()) {
    Thread.yield();  // hint: let other threads make progress
    // Better alternative: Thread.onSpinWait() (Java 9)
    // Even better: use wait/notify or BlockingQueue!
}

// 2. Testing — force context switches to expose race conditions:
void buggyIncrement() {
    int temp = counter;
    Thread.yield();  // increase chance of context switch HERE
    counter = temp + 1;
    // With yield: race condition is MORE LIKELY to manifest in tests
}

// ═══ BETTER ALTERNATIVES ═══
// Instead of yield():
// Thread.sleep(1)           — guaranteed pause (at least 1ms)
// Thread.onSpinWait()       — Java 9, CPU hint for spin loops (efficient)
// LockSupport.parkNanos(1)  — minimal park (nanosecond precision)
// BlockingQueue.take()      — proper wait-for-data mechanism
// Condition.await()         — proper wait-for-condition mechanism

// ═══ yield() vs sleep(0) vs onSpinWait() ═══
// yield():       OS scheduler hint, may be ignored
// sleep(0):      similar to yield on some OSes, gives up timeslice
// onSpinWait():  Java 9, CPU-level hint (PAUSE instruction on x86)
//                tells CPU "I'm spinning, reduce power/let other hyperthread run"
//                BEST for spin loops!
